= Improvements over the original GCAD

The reference is Vitaly Magerya's GCAD (a Python/SymPy front end over FLINT). It implements Strzeboński's 2000 _generic_ CAD: generic projection (leading coefficients, discriminants and pairwise resultants of a square-free basis), recursive lifting with exact real-root isolation, the MODS/SOTD/T1 ordering heuristics and an aggressive cell merge. Everything listed below goes beyond that. The list is meant to be exhaustive: every engine, certificate family, projection or kernel change, local-engine mechanism, ordering method, cache, verifier feature, supervision feature, interface, upstream fix and contract extension that exists in the code, plus the investigated ideas that were not adopted.

*Vocabulary.* A CAD splits the solution set into _cells_. _Projection_ eliminates variables one at a time and collects the polynomials (resultants, discriminants, leading coefficients) whose zeros may change the picture; _lifting_ goes back up, picking rational _sample_ points and isolating real roots above them. The _order_ fixes which variable is eliminated first. A _certificate_ is an exact, replayable proof (e.g. that a polynomial never vanishes on the domain) that lets a polynomial be dropped. A _guard_ is a condition that must hold for a local shortcut to be valid. The _anchor_ of a homogeneous problem is the positive variable that is fixed to 1. _Generic_ means correct outside an explicit finite list of lower-dimensional exceptional sets. An _engine_ is a strategy that turns the problem into one or more smaller CAD problems and rebuilds the original cells with a proof. A _stack_ is the list of cells above one cell of the lower-dimensional decomposition: the real roots of the relevant polynomials over that cell cut its cylinder into _sections_ (the root graphs themselves) and _sectors_ (the open slices between consecutive roots). A polynomial is _delineable_ over a cell when its real roots keep their number and order everywhere above that cell, so they can serve as stack boundaries. A _seam_ is a boundary between two neighbouring cells on which the target keeps the same sign; GCAD's merge may remove it, symGCAD keeps it (V4).

*Tags.* #inv-on active without any setting (for a mechanism of an opt-in engine, e.g. the local engine: active whenever that engine runs); #inv-opt enabled by an option, engine or ordering choice (the option name is given); #inv-patch optional dependency patch, never in the default build; #inv-diag measurement or audit tool, not part of a solve; #inv-contract changes what a result means and must be requested explicitly; #inv-new written in the 2026-10-12 session.

*Reading the numbers.* Unless a line says otherwise, an effect is one measured case or operand: it shows what the change does, not a corpus-wide speedup (corpus-wide timings are in Section 1, kernel-level comparisons with FLINT in Section 3). "Timeout" results never count as solved. All reductions keep the exact default contract: every cell is proved, every interior `F=0` sheet and same-sign seam stays a boundary. The only exception is the opt-in contract extension X1, which must be switched on explicitly and is never counted in the strict totals.

