== Exact algebra and kernels

- #inv-id[A1] *Rust and Symbolica exact backend.* GCAD drives FLINT through Python and SymPy; symGCAD does all polynomial arithmetic, GCDs, factorization and resultants in Rust on Symbolica, inside single-threaded supervised worker processes. No floating-point number enters any proof. #inv-eff in the matched parity audit (release `9025e71`, identical orders, plain `baseline` projection), symGCAD reproduced the raw cell counts of all 483 sign-expanded problems GCAD completes; projection + lifting + merging took a geometric mean of 0.1466 of GCAD's time (6.82× faster), whole processes 0.0620 (16.1×). The current end-to-end comparison is in Section 1. #inv-on
- #inv-id[A2] *Sign-preserving exact input normalization.* Every inequality becomes "this polynomial is positive"; denominators and rational content are cleared without ever flipping a sign. The parser tells an empty problem (`false`, a strict zero constraint) apart from a legitimate identically zero split, and it refuses exponent overflow, undeclared symbols and approximate (floating-point) kinematics instead of guessing. #inv-on
- #inv-id[A3] *Full variable maps.* Every polynomial keeps its complete ordered variable list, including variables it does not depend on. A cached result computed under one ordering or coordinate chart can therefore never be reused for another one that merely looks the same. #inv-on
- #inv-id[A4] *Deterministic resultants.* Symbolica's automatic resultant dispatcher may accept a modular (CRT) answer after random-prime checks, which is not a proof. Production and verification use the deterministic integer Lazard–Ducos subresultant algorithm (plus the exact kernels A6–A9); the dispatcher is confined to profiling, and caches built with it were invalidated. #inv-eff on large captured operands Symbolica's Ducos runs at 0.74–0.88× the time of FLINT's Ducos (Section 3). #inv-on
- #inv-id[A5] *Closed-form low-degree discriminants.* The discriminant of a quadratic, cubic or quartic is evaluated from its exact invariant formula instead of the generic `Res(p, p')`, which expands a large Sylvester determinant. #inv-eff captured quartic (642 → 69,474 terms): 2.71 s / 48 MiB against 29.77 s / 472 MiB (10.98× time, 9.83× memory); on captured kinematic discriminants the production path is 6–18× faster than FLINT 3.6 (Section 3). #inv-on
- #inv-id[A6] *Exact evaluation/interpolation resultants.* When a resultant or discriminant depends on one remaining parameter, it is computed at many integer values of that parameter (each a plain integer resultant) and rebuilt by exact interpolation; a proved degree bound says how many values suffice, so the answer is exact, not probabilistic. #inv-eff captured symbolic A446 pair (degrees 23 and 17, 588/387 terms): 5.888 s with 872 images; every Ducos variant exceeded 60 s. #inv-on
- #inv-id[A7] *Assignment degree bound and size-derived admission.* The number of interpolation points is the smaller of the closed-form Sylvester degree bounds and an exact assignment (Hungarian algorithm) bound on the Sylvester matrix, which is often much tighter. Plans outside the historical envelope (closed-form bound ≤ 1,024), up to 65,536, are admitted only if an a-priori bit bound on every image fits the caps, so the kernel cannot run out of budget after it has been chosen. #inv-eff degree-1,828 discriminant: 27.80 s (old multivariate-image interpolation 52.31 s, old default path > 660 s); degree-2,220: 58.83 s against 106.79 s. #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[A8] *Exact integer images with a zero-image guard.* Each interpolation image is an exact integer resultant; an exact gcd test first decides whether it is zero, because Symbolica 3.0.1's Lazard–Ducos panics on a vanishing subresultant (U3). The same guard protects the full Ducos path. #inv-eff a pair on which the previous release crashed now solves (7 cells) and verifies. #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[A9] *Binary (Kronecker) segmentation.* For two-parameter resultants and discriminants, the parameters are packed into one integer variable by substituting large powers of two under proved degree and coefficient bounds; one univariate integer resultant then replaces a multivariate computation, and the result is unpacked with complete signed checks. #inv-eff authentic A317/A318 resultants of 9,835/18,939 terms in 38.16/20.03 s; forced on a 56-degree-product discriminant 2.17 s against Ducos 7.80 s; on the captured large resultants it is 3–16× faster than FLINT 3.6's best kernel (Section 3). Default since 2026-10-12 (`binary_resultants = false` opts out). #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[A10] *Second-route spot check.* Because A6–A9 are new code paths, every interpolated or segmented result is re-evaluated at one extra full-degree point through a different Symbolica route (substitution, then Brown's resultant); a disagreement is a hard failure. This runs in the solver and again in the verifier. #inv-eff cost within noise (27.86 s against 27.87 s). #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[A11] *Sum-variable factor preconditioning.* A polynomial that depends on two variables only through their sum is factored as a polynomial in that sum (one variable fewer) and mapped back; the inverse substitution and the factor product are re-checked. #inv-eff a stalled A446 factorization (native > 60 s) took 0.003–0.025 s. #inv-on
- #inv-id[A12] *Affine factorization preconditioning.* Multivariate factorization can stall when the polynomial is degenerate at the evaluation point the factorizer picks. A bounded exact integer translation first moves the polynomial to a regular fiber; the factors are mapped back and their signed product is rebuilt to prove that nothing was lost. #inv-eff 224-term Gluza33 factor 1.63 s, where the native call timed out at 30.02 s; one operand showed about 1.42× overhead, so it stays opt-in. #inv-opt (`affine_factorization`)
- #inv-id[A13] *Support-preserving diagonal scaling.* Tried first inside the affine policy: multiplying variables by nonzero constants reaches a regular fiber without creating new monomials (a translation can blow up the term count). #inv-eff a 1,283-term factor on which translation stalled for 30 s factors in 0.048 s. #inv-opt (inside `affine_factorization`)
- #inv-id[A14] *Bounded exact shear.* Third strategy of the affine policy: the substitution `x -> x + c·y` for a small integer `c`, with an exact inverse, for polynomials on which translation and scaling fail. #inv-eff captured C470 obstruction 0.088 s; the original C470 continuation then saved 56,671 more exact operations (still incomplete). #inv-opt (inside `affine_factorization`)
- #inv-id[A15] *Primitive parameter-linear irreducibility shortcut.* A polynomial that is linear in one variable and whose coefficients share no common factor is irreducible (Gauss's lemma), so the factorizer is not called at all. #inv-eff 4.840 → 0.120 ms and 1.463 → 0.079 ms on two core inputs. #inv-on

== Root isolation and lifting

- #inv-id[L1] *Algebraic cell boundaries, re-isolated at every prefix.* A boundary is stored as "the k-th root of polynomial p", not as a number. At each new rational prefix its roots are isolated exactly again; coincident roots are separated by exact gcds and interval refinement under a work bound. The proof records the boundaries, so the verifier can recheck every one. #inv-on
- #inv-id[L2] *Open-interval Sturm counting.* Real roots on open or unbounded intervals are counted with signed primitive pseudo-remainder sequences, which keep coefficients small (Symbolica alone counts only on `(0, ∞)`). #inv-eff degree-12 A381 guard 3.184 → 0.602 ms, largest coefficient 2,281 → 555 bits. #inv-on
- #inv-id[L3] *Integer sign evaluation.* The sign of a polynomial at a rational point is computed by clearing the denominator and running Horner's rule on integers, instead of rational arithmetic with a gcd at every step. #inv-eff 2.64–11.01× in a microbenchmark. #inv-on
- #inv-id[L4] *Integer prefix evaluation.* The same idea for substituting a whole rational prefix into a multivariate polynomial: exact integer numerators with one common denominator. #inv-eff guard batches 3.79× (A446) and 5.33× (A381); the matched whole-solver control moved < 1% (43.68 → 43.30 s). #inv-on
- #inv-id[L5] *Single-pass batched specialization.* All coordinates of a prefix are substituted in one pass that shares the powers of each coordinate, instead of one variable after another. #inv-eff 2,000 prefixes of `ex_e5/2`: 0.401 → 0.252 s; `ex38`: 0.079 → 0.052 s. #inv-on
- #inv-id[L6] *Deferred GCDs and borrowed root signs.* GCDs that are often unnecessary are postponed until a collision actually has to be resolved, and signs at a root reuse data already computed during isolation. #inv-eff improved the three cases where symGCAD was slowest relative to GCAD in the parity audit; their time ratios remain 2.09, 1.61 and 2.75. #inv-on
- #inv-id[L7] *Primitive shared root fibers.* All root intervals of one specialized polynomial share one immutable defining polynomial; retained rational fibers are made primitive integer polynomials; affine prefix guards skip power tables. Less copying and smaller numbers during lifting. #inv-eff A414 fixed-work traversal 7.748 → 6.917 s (−10.7%; a first pair was 5.3% slower). #inv-on
- #inv-id[L8] *Ordered root union.* When the roots of many polynomials have to be merged into one sorted list of boundaries, a priority queue merges them, with exact equality and bisection checks so that equal or extremely close roots land in the right order. No speedup established; it is the correctness backbone of LP3. #inv-on
- #inv-id[L9] *Positive and descending root selectors.* A boundary may be "the k-th positive root" (`positive`) or "the k-th root counted from the largest" (`real_descending`). On a positive domain this keeps its meaning when irrelevant negative roots collide or disappear. Only certified rules produce such selectors, and the verifier rechecks them. #inv-on (produced only by certified rules)

== Projection operators and certified reductions

- #inv-id[P1] *Faithful generic projection.* The `baseline` engine reproduces Strzeboński's projection and lifting exactly; it is the reference for A1 and the fallback for every engine. #inv-on (`engine = "baseline"`)
- #inv-id[P2] *Optimized projection engine.* The same projection, but every exact operation is cached under a canonical key and reused across levels, ordering trials and restarts (K1–K3), and generated factors may be removed with certificates (P3). Most strict corpus rows run it, directly or as a base engine. #inv-opt (`engine = "optimized"`)
- #inv-id[P3] *Certified factor removal.* A generated projection factor is dropped only with an exact certificate (C1–C5) that it is strictly nonzero on the domain; nonnegativity is not enough, because a zero sheet inside the domain would be lost. Original constraints and requested splits stay protected; `input_positivity` extends the rule to input factors. #inv-eff fixed A446: 1.326 s with it, timeout at 60 s without. #inv-opt (`positivity`, `input_positivity`)
- #inv-id[P4] *Operation pruning.* Tries a nonvanishing proof before factoring a whole projection operation, so a provably irrelevant resultant is never expanded. Off by default because a failed proof costs time. #inv-opt (`prune_operations`)
- #inv-id[P5] *Strict-Bézout pair omission in global projection.* A supplied identity `p*L + q*M = k*S` with `k > 0`, where `S = H^2` with `H` certified nonzero or `S` certified positive, proves that `p` and `q` have no common zero in the box, so `Res(p, q)` is skipped. `p`, `q`, their leading coefficients and discriminants stay, face pairs are never skipped, and every omission is replayed by the verifier. #inv-eff Gluza33 piece `R--`, third level: 1,033 → 661 polynomials and 228,998 → 112,759 terms (−36%, −51%), reached at 122.4 s instead of 164.2 s. #inv-new #inv-opt (`projection_strict_bezout_hints`)

== Positivity and domain certificates

- #inv-id[C1] *Exact nonvanishing certificate portfolio.* A certificate proves that a polynomial is strictly positive (or negative) on the whole box of interest, so it can never be a cell boundary there. Cheap tests come first: coefficient signs, positive coordinate shifts, Pólya and Bernstein expansions, univariate Sturm counts on open and unbounded domains and exact rules for quadratics, each under a work bound. Every success is serialized into the proof and replayed by the verifier on the original domain. #inv-on (used when `positivity` is set)
- #inv-id[C2] *Parameterized quadratics.* A quadratic whose discriminant is proved negative on the whole domain (with a positive leading coefficient) is positive there. #inv-on (inside C1)
- #inv-id[C3] *Square plus positive residual.* Writes a polynomial as an exact square (of a monomial or an affine form, also after a homogeneous scale is fixed) plus a strictly positive residual, with oriented bounds. #inv-on (inside C1)
- #inv-id[C4] *Weighted-square certificates.* Sums of binomial squares with positive monomial weights plus a positive residual, for polynomials that are positive but have mixed-sign coefficients. #inv-eff "positive endpoint" case: 20 → 1 cell, 37.2 → 9.3 ms solver time. #inv-opt (`weighted_square_positivity`)
- #inv-id[C5] *Positive-semidefinite quadratic components.* Exact rational LDL elimination of quadratic subforms plus a strictly positive residual; no floating-point eigenvalues. #inv-eff full quadratic constant 8 → 1 cell (18.3 → 7.6 ms); local linear split 12 → 2 cells, with the interior zero sheet kept. #inv-opt (`quadratic_component_positivity`)
- #inv-id[C6] *Caller-chosen certificate budgets.* Library callers may raise the limits above the defaults of 4,096 Bernstein coefficients and 10#super[6] work units (symGCAD PR \#2 allowed up to 262,144 and 10#super[9]\; C9 raises these ceilings); the chosen limits are written into the proof and used again by the verifier. #inv-opt (library `CertificateOptions`)
- #inv-id[C7] *Early abandonment from exact samples.* An exact zero or a sign change found at a sample point ends a hopeless certificate attempt immediately. Samples only ever stop a search; they never prove anything. #inv-on
- #inv-id[C8] *Certified local guards.* In the local engine, a certificate can replace a generated guard polynomial; the certificate's own premises become protected obligations of the ancestor cell. #inv-eff fixed A446 local: 8 certificates replaced 436 guard occurrences; 35 cells in 0.427 s, where the unreduced run timed out at 60 s. #inv-opt (`positivity` with `engine = "local"`)
- #inv-id[C9] *Larger explicit certificate allowances.* The C6 ceilings rise to 2,097,152 Bernstein coefficients and 6.4 × 10#super[10] work units (defaults unchanged). The tensor size is checked before allocation, and the work allowance is validated and debited without overflow. #inv-eff regression tensors of 263,169 (two variables) and 1,115,370 coefficients (eight variables), refused under the earlier ceilings, now give exact certificates that replay from their saved options; lowered saved limits are rejected. #inv-new #inv-opt (library `CertificateOptions`)

== Composite engines

- #inv-id[E1] *Homogeneous positive-scale reduction.* Feynman-parameter polynomials are homogeneous in a block of positive variables: scaling the whole block does not change any sign. One proved-positive anchor coordinate is therefore fixed to 1, the smaller problem is solved, and the original cells are rebuilt exactly. #inv-eff fixed A446 2.409 → 1.326 s, fixed Ax12 1.032 → 1.445 s (mixed, hence opt-in). #inv-opt (`homogeneous_block`, `homogeneous_anchor`)
- #inv-id[E2] *Monotone engine.* If certificates prove that `F` strictly increases along a positive axis, the solution set along that axis is one interval ending at a root: the axis is removed, the endpoint problem is solved, and every root sector is restored with its proof. #inv-eff fixed A446/Ax12: 35 → 8 cells; A446 1.326 → 0.825 s. #inv-opt (`engine = "monotone"`, base engine `monotone_base_engine`)
- #inv-id[E3] *Conditional quadratic peel.* When `F` is quadratic in an axis, positive-multiplier certificates justify consecutive endpoint reductions on each side of the vertex. #inv-eff fixed A381 and Ax381: 836 verified cells each; in this report's cold runs (Section 1) #inv-sv("finiteA_7_381_10_0__4_0_1_1_1_1_1_0_1/fixed") and #inv-sv("finiteAx12_7_381_10_0__4_0_1_1_1_1_1_0_1/fixed") (earlier matched control, build `0a95bc2`, medians of 3: 0.186 and 0.102 s solve); all 6 trials without the rule timed out at 60 s. #inv-opt (inside `monotone`)
- #inv-id[E4] *Negative-target mode.* Solves `Q > 0` as the negative side of `F = -Q` through its endpoint inequality, restoring only the interval below the positive root. #inv-eff full original physical elliptic problem (`test_elliptic2l_physical_symb/order/1`): 269,061 cells; this report's cold run #inv-sv("test_elliptic2l_physical_symb/order/1") (first release run, build `1e29f04`: 33.86 s / 73.09 s). #inv-opt (`monotone_negative_target`)
- #inv-id[E5] *Sign filter.* For a problem without a requested split, decomposes the full sign arrangement of one non-affine constraint and keeps exactly the required cells, with an exhaustive selection map that the verifier checks. #inv-opt (inside `monotone`)
- #inv-id[E6] *Linear-negative engine.* A target linear in `z` with `A*z + B < 0` is reduced to `A < 0`, using a guide polynomial whose positivity and square identity `B*C - A*D = k*H^2` are proved; the lower boundary is the largest root of `z*q`. #inv-eff negative Gluza30 (`test_gluza_ex30/order/1`): 1 cell, #inv-sv("test_gluza_ex30/order/1")#[;] negative Gluza33 (`test_gluza_ex33/order/1`): 3 cells, #inv-sv("test_gluza_ex33/order/1") (this report's cold runs; earlier release medians of 3, build `256527b`: 0.185 s and 0.164 s solve); GCAD timed out at 60 s on both in those earlier controls. #inv-opt (`engine = "linear_negative"`, `negative_linear_guide`)
- #inv-id[E7] *Bilinear-positive engine.* For `a*x*y + b*x + c*y + d > 0` on the last two positive axes, seven strict certificates against a guide polynomial fix the shape of the two-dimensional fiber; only the base (the remaining variables) is decomposed, over the signs of `a`, `b`, `c`. #inv-eff positive Gluza30: 80,980 verified cells; this report's cold run #inv-sv("test_gluza_ex30/solve/3") (release controls, build `ff22294`, medians of 3: 22.57 s / 16.85 s); GCAD timed out. Under the opt-in contract X1 the same problem needs 1 cell. #inv-opt (`engine = "bilinear_positive"`, `bilinear_positive_guide`, `bilinear_positive_base_engine`)
- #inv-id[E8] *General bilinear engine with pieces.* Handles all 16 sign patterns of `a, b, c, d`: ratio premises between them are found from the Symanzik guide by square-free decomposition, a separator `G` satisfies `b*c - a*d = ±m*G^2`, and a planner splits the base into independently solvable pieces plus proved-empty regions (objective `region_weighted`, the default, or `atoms`). The verifier checks the partition by a truth table and verifies every piece. #inv-eff Gluza33: a plan of 4 pieces + 1 empty claim in 0.034 s; a 4-piece synthetic chain solved as 4 separate processes and assembled gave 47 verified cells (pieces CLI test); on a different synthetic input, a 3-parameter quartic chain with the local base engine, `region_weighted` was 4–14% faster than `atoms` (8,142 against 12,015 cells); under the strict contract the four Gluza33 pieces still time out (1,200 s); with X1 the engine completes positive Gluza33. #inv-new #inv-opt (`engine = "bilinear_general"`, `bilinear_general_guide`, `bilinear_general_plan_objective`)
- #inv-id[E9] *Quadratic ladder.* When a quadratic opens two positive roots below a linear fiber, restores a proved five-sector arrangement over a verified base, keeping both sides of `F = 0`. #inv-eff crossed A446 chamber `t < 4`: 5 verified cells in 0.022 s (debug); the unrestricted problem stays unresolved. #inv-opt (`engine = "quadratic_ladder"`)
- #inv-id[E10] *Selectable base engines and inner anchors.* Monotone and bilinear wrappers solve their base with `baseline`, `optimized` or `local`, optionally with an inner homogeneous anchor and a block that keeps kinematic parameters outside the scale block. #inv-opt (`monotone_base_engine`, `bilinear_*_base_engine`, `bilinear_general_base_anchor`; `bilinear_general_base_block` is #inv-new)
- #inv-id[E11] *Projection-free peel inspection.* Reports which monotone peels are certifiable for an input without solving anything, to choose recipes cheaply. #inv-diag

== Local projection engine

- #inv-id[LP1] *Local projection.* Ordinary CAD projects every pair of polynomials everywhere. The local engine, an adaptation of Strzeboński's local projection, projects only what the sample points it actually visits need: it starts with few cuts, learns the missing ancestor cuts a stack requires and rebuilds the affected stacks. The whole conditional tree is checked by the independent verifier. Discovery order matters, which is what LP12, LP19 and LP22 address. #inv-opt (`engine = "local"`)
- #inv-id[LP2] *Batched sibling discovery.* Collects new obligations from several sibling stacks before rebuilding their parent, instead of rebuilding after each one; a tentative stack with new cuts is always discarded, only a fully saturated pass produces cells. #inv-on (`local_batch_siblings = false` restores one-sibling discovery)
- #inv-id[LP3] *Direct first-axis base.* On the first lifting axis, univariate cuts need no pair resultants: the union of their real roots is isolated directly (with L8), and the verifier checks this zero-dimensional base condition. #inv-eff a fixed A381 run avoided 4,150,168 potential first-axis pairs. #inv-on (`local_base_point_projection`)
- #inv-id[LP4] *Positive-ray Descartes rule.* On a proved-positive axis, zero or one sign variation in the coefficients proves "no positive root" or "exactly one simple positive root", replacing the discriminant. #inv-on (`local_descartes_one`)
- #inv-id[LP5] *Quadratic positive-ray classifier with cached guards.* Exact sign cases of a quadratic's coefficients decide its positive roots; guard results are cached across stacks. #inv-eff fixed-work A381 debug controls: solver time −15.0% and −14.5% (process wall time mixed). #inv-on
- #inv-id[LP6] *Leading-term cancellation separation.* Up to two exact cancellations whose coefficients all have one certified sign prove that two polynomials share no positive root, so their pair resultant is skipped; inconclusive cases keep it. #inv-on (`local_pair_cancellations = 2`)
- #inv-id[LP7] *Adjacent-root pairs.* Projects only the pairs whose roots are adjacent at the sample. #inv-eff no completion gain: all 27 symbolic release attempts timed out. #inv-opt (`local_adjacent_pairs`)
- #inv-id[LP8] *Descartes witnesses for 2–8 positive roots.* Rational separators with alternating signs, plus coefficient guards, prove that each interval holds exactly one simple positive root, replacing a large discriminant. At most two templates per polynomial keep the guard set finite; the source size budget is 1–65,536 terms. #inv-eff with 8,192 terms, the original Gluza33 passed a 6,938-term degree-16 discriminant and saved 4,554 more operations. #inv-opt (`local_descartes_witnesses`, `local_descartes_witness_max_terms`)
- #inv-id[LP9] *Interval Descartes covers.* Covers the positive ray by rational intervals with zero or one sign variation each, for fibers where the global Descartes count is not exact. #inv-eff Gluza33 19,942-term degree-24 discriminant (2 roots, 4 variations) bypassed with 49 guards (factored in 9.27 s): +6,422 operations; a 131,072-term aggregate budget gave +7,194; template limit 4 gave +1,094 and +2,397. #inv-opt (`local_descartes_intervals`, `local_descartes_interval_max_terms`, `..._max_total_terms` (default 65,536, ceiling 262,144), `..._template_limit`)
- #inv-id[LP10] *Adjacent interval coalescing.* Merges neighbouring cover intervals when an exact recount keeps at most one variation, which shrinks the guard set. #inv-eff four-root cover with 97 guards / 109,726 terms built in 1.60 s; the greedy cover needed 121 / 139,654, over budget. #inv-on (when intervals are enabled)
- #inv-id[LP11] *Positive pair witnesses.* Once both polynomials' positive roots are proved delineable, rational separators between them replace the pair resultant. Sample-dependent, without a termination proof; caused both measured thrash cases (LP22). #inv-opt (`local_positive_pair_witnesses`)
- #inv-id[LP12] *Pair-witness admission gate and axis budget.* A new separator template is admitted only when the resultant it replaces would be large (a dense term bound above a threshold), and per-axis template counts can be capped; otherwise the resultant, always admissible, is used. #inv-eff Gluza33 base piece `a > 0` with witnesses: 20.4 million nodes in 570 s without finishing → 0.02 s and 178 verified cells at gate 4,096; on 19 of 20 corpus cases the gate behaved like witnesses off. Default 0 keeps the old policy. #inv-new #inv-opt (`local_pair_witness_min_resultant_terms`, `local_pair_witness_axis_budget`)
- #inv-id[LP13] *Local strict-Bézout hints.* The local counterpart of P5 for chosen cut pairs. #inv-eff positive Gluza30: 49 hints skipped 2,918 resultants (no completion). #inv-opt (`local_strict_bezout_hints`)
- #inv-id[LP14] *Completed-subtree reuse.* A complete conditional proof below a rational prefix is reused when the same prefix comes back, after its obligations are rechecked; budget 32 MiB, entries of 128–4,096 nodes. #inv-eff C470 at an equal refinement limit 8.40 → 6.96 s with 1,024-node entries; no full-solve gain. #inv-on (`local_reuse_subtrees`, `local_subtree_cache_mib`, `local_subtree_cache_entry_nodes`)
- #inv-id[LP15] *Learned-cut hints.* Unfinished stacks keep the cuts they learned as hints for the next visit (8 MiB); hints are always re-projected, never trusted. #inv-on (`local_reuse_learned_cuts`, `local_learned_cut_cache_mib`)
- #inv-id[LP16] *Persistent hint archive.* Saves cut hints across restarts (8 MiB), re-checked on load. #inv-eff no completion gain in matched restarts. #inv-opt (`local_hint_archive`)
- #inv-id[LP17] *Stable samples.* Keeps a rational sample across refinements when an exact comparison places it strictly inside a new sector, so caches keyed by the sample still hit. #inv-opt (`local_stable_samples`)
- #inv-id[LP18] *Deferred projection with cut compaction.* Discovers complete children before projecting, then drops learned cuts that no child needs. #inv-eff A414 pilot still timed out at 300 s. #inv-opt (`local_deferred_projection`)
- #inv-id[LP19] *Retained sibling proofs.* Completed children survive a stack's refinement rounds: cuts only grow at a fixed prefix, so each child moves to the new sector that strictly contains its unchanged sample (checked exactly against the roots). Retained pools share a quarter of the memory limit at 4 KiB per node. #inv-eff Gluza33 piece `Ppm`, 30 min: 12.80 → 1.38 million speculative nodes for the same progress (still incomplete); fixed `B6_469` (witness recipe, gate 4,096) completes only with retention: 112,124 verified cells in 9.91 s. Stays opt-in because it changes samples and hence cell counts. #inv-new #inv-opt (`local_retain_siblings`, requires `local_stable_samples`)
- #inv-id[LP20] *First-axis square-free support basis.* Replaces irreducible factorization of bounded generated first-axis guards by checked square-free blocks, whose product identities the verifier replays. #inv-eff 92.06 s against 185.83 s on one A377 block set. #inv-opt (`local_base_univariate_basis`)
- #inv-id[LP21] *Discovery caps.* `local_max_refinements` (10,000) and `local_max_nodes` (100,000) bound speculative work; exhaustion returns `incomplete`, never a partial answer. #inv-on
- #inv-id[LP22] *Refinement-thrash diagnostics.* Read-only counters of restarts, cut origins, discarded children and cache-miss causes; a test checks that proofs and counters are identical with it on and off. #inv-eff found 337,116 completed children discarded in 60 s on one Gluza33 piece, which motivated LP12 and LP19. #inv-new #inv-diag (`SYMGCAD_LOCAL_THRASH_DIAG=1`)
- #inv-id[LP23] *Obstruction observer.* Records which guard fails at an exact prefix without changing proofs, to locate the next bottleneck. #inv-diag

== Ordering and search

- #inv-id[O1] *Extra ordering heuristics.* Brown and sparsity orders join GCAD's MODS, SOTD and T1; `variable_groups` and explicit orders bind every candidate, so parameters stay outside the integration variables. #inv-opt (`ordering`)
- #inv-id[O2] *Bounded MCTS order search.* Monte-Carlo tree search (UCT) over legal elimination prefixes: seed 0, at most 256 rollouts, search time `min(300 s, 20%)` of the limit, promotion tiers of 1/10/60 s; only a completed exact solve can win. #inv-eff 126/126 cold ablation solves verified, but no end-to-end gain over the cheap heuristics (details in the separate ordering/MCTS companion). #inv-opt (`ordering = "mcts"`, `search_time_secs`, `max_rollouts`)
- #inv-id[O3] *Search pruning.* Abandons ordering trials whose completed prefixes already show severe term growth. It is a prediction that only saves search time; it never rejects a region and never affects the final solve. #inv-on (with MCTS; `search_pruning`)
- #inv-id[O4] *Local-progress promotion score.* Ranks timed-out local trials by polynomial inventory and refinement growth, so the most promising order gets the longer budget. #inv-eff reduced positive Gluza30 base: 3/3 verified, median 65.10 s, against 3/3 incomplete at 313.63 s. #inv-opt (`search_local_progress`)
- #inv-id[O5] *Kinematic portfolio ordering.* For parametric Feynman inputs the cost is driven by the number of cells in the kinematic variables. The portfolio probes the default MODS order, every certified homogeneous anchor and every kinematic order in killable workers, counts the exact open kinematic cells of each, and solves with the fewest (written back as `ordering = "given"`, so solver and verifier are unchanged); decoupled kinematic variables are lifted last. #inv-eff 26 solved cases: 105.88 → 92.97 s total; C531 21.22 → 2.27 s (11,413 → 36 kinematic cells); 4 held-out cases where the default ran out of 5 GiB now solve (5.7–47.6 s); losses `Cx12_438` 32.30 → 59.82 s and 36 tiny cases 2.07 → 7.73 s; closes none of A317, A318, C231. #inv-new #inv-opt (`ordering = "kinematic_portfolio"`, `portfolio_*` budgets, `portfolio_kinematic_cell_cap` 1,000,000, `portfolio_fiber_samples` 8)
- #inv-id[O6] *Reproducible portfolio budgets.* Fixed per-candidate budgets give the same choice on 21/21 cases on a loaded host, at higher cost (1,317 s on the 26 cases). #inv-new #inv-opt (`portfolio_fixed_budgets`)
- #inv-id[O7] *Order proposal helpers.* Support-order seeds and short linear-prefix probes; measured, no gain. #inv-diag

== Caching and checkpointing

- #inv-id[K1] *Exact operation cache.* Factorizations, resultants, discriminants and completed projection prefixes are shared across stages and ordering trials, so a second order that shares a prefix pays nothing for it (128 MiB). #inv-on (optimized and local engines; `cache_memory_mib`)
- #inv-id[K2] *Persistent checkpoints.* Atomic, hash-validated on-disk records of every completed exact operation survive interruptions mid-stage, so a long campaign can resume. #inv-eff the final original Gluza33 campaign retained 32,456 operations (4,559 new) for continuation. #inv-opt (`checkpoint_dir`; automatic with MCTS)
- #inv-id[K3] *Domain-free operation keys.* Pure algebra records are keyed by variable map, axis, backend version and exact input, no longer by the domain, so different region pieces of one problem share algebra; old records are validated and migrated. #inv-eff Gluza33 `R--` after `R+-`: 50 of 127 operations from disk through level 2 (old scheme: 0). #inv-new #inv-on (with `checkpoint_dir`)
- #inv-id[K4] *Primitive-linear cache bypass.* Trivially irreducible factors (A15) skip cache input/output entirely. #inv-eff 30 polynomials, fresh memory and disk caches: 15.531 → 0.415 ms. #inv-on
- #inv-id[K5] *Search journal.* MCTS state (8 MiB, 4,096 records) is replayed for the same problem and executable after a restart; replayed claims count as unverified until re-solved. #inv-on (with MCTS)
- #inv-id[K6] *Untrusted proposal caches.* First-axis support proposals and hint archives are re-factored and re-checked on load; the verifier never reads any cache. #inv-opt
- #inv-id[K7] *Cache integrity audits.* Inherited bytes, operation keys and output hashes are checked before a continuation reuses work. #inv-diag

== Verification and evidence

- #inv-id[V1] *Explicit generic-coverage contract.* A result proves that its cells lie in the solution set `S` and cover `S` up to the zero set of an explicit finite list of nonzero exceptional polynomials, and that the cells are disjoint. GCAD returns cells without such a list. #inv-on
- #inv-id[V2] *Self-contained JSON proof.* Records the normalized problem, the actual order, the polynomial table, projection levels, rational samples, root-index boundaries, an exhaustive tree of stacks, rejections and accepted cells, and one of four statuses (`complete_generic`, `incomplete`, `invalid_input`, `computation_failure`). #inv-on
- #inv-id[V3] *Independent verifier.* `symgcad verify` re-normalizes the original input, rebuilds every projection and reduction obligation, checks roots with exact arithmetic and walks every open interval; every engine has its own typed replay; an incomplete result cannot pass. Every verified row of Section 1 passed it in a separate process. #inv-on
- #inv-id[V4] *Raw cells and seams by default.* No merging unless asked: interior `F = 0` sheets and same-sign seams always remain boundaries (GCAD merges aggressively, and some of its merges cross such seams). #inv-on
- #inv-id[V5] *Indexed compatibility merge.* GCAD-style merging as a separate output that records which seams were removed. #inv-eff the 131,256-cell `ex38` now completes (median 40.72 s, 4.77 s merging; GCAD 26.50/7.45 s). #inv-opt (`merge = "aggressive"`)
- #inv-id[V6] *Replay hardening for the new rules.* Strict-Bézout omissions (11 tampered variants rejected), `bilinear_general` evidence (about 40 forgeries rejected), the assignment degree bound (checked through its LP dual), projective plans (X1) and unresolved portfolio inputs are all checked or refused by the verifier. #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[V7] *Certified comparison of decompositions.* Exact common refinement of two results, across different orders and reductions, to show that two proofs describe the same set. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[V8] *Seeded interior sanity check.* Draws fresh rational points in every cell (seed 20261010, two per cell) and evaluates the original TOML expressions exactly; outside all timings and no proof credit. #inv-eff fixed A446: 8 cells, 16 `F` and 112 constraint evaluations, 0 failures, 0.41 s; positive Gluza33 under X1: 4,744 points, 0 failures. #inv-diag (`examples/sanity_sample.rs`)
- #inv-id[V9] *Native comparison harness and parity audit.* Frozen GCAD cohort, three paired runs, adaptive native cap `max(300 s, 3 × symGCAD solve)`; documents GCAD merge and assertion defects (e.g. `failing_test_ex43`). #inv-diag
- #inv-id[V10] *Signed grid verification of huge algebra.* The A377 degree-32 discriminant candidate (degree 4,058, 3,261 terms) was proved by 4,773 exact evaluation points in 2,093.9 s. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[V11] *Original-identity audit.* Every claimed completion is re-checked against the original polynomials, domain, groups and order: 303 of 331 distinct problems (124/124 fixed ZZ, 97/124 symbolic ZZ, 82/83 core), re-confirmed by the cold runs of Section 1. #inv-diag

== Robustness and supervision

- #inv-id[R1] *Killable worker processes and limits.* Solves, verifications and probes run in separate process groups with hard wall-time and memory limits (defaults 1 h, 64 GiB, 16 workers; up to 5 h on request, R11) and an optional cell cap, so a blocking native call cannot hang a run and a timeout is reported, not lost. #inv-on (`[limits]`: `wall_time_secs`, `memory_mib`, `workers`, `max_cells`)
- #inv-id[R2] *Campaign and slow-suite runners.* `symgcad bench` and the resumable one-hour slow queue record wall, CPU and RSS; timeouts stay incomplete. #inv-on
- #inv-id[R3] *Fast regression suite.* 759 tests in 59 test targets pass after the all-in-main consolidation (0 failed, 2 expensive tests ignored, 350 s summed test time); they include exact rationals and signs, proof mutations, same-sign seams, repeated and nearby roots, degree drops and worker termination. #inv-eff grew from 662 at `81d9ac5` (October 11) to 753 at `9baf91c` with the 2026-10-12 work, then to 759 with the C9 tests and the A381 `mz2=1/2` regression. #inv-on (`scripts/test.sh fast --locked`)
- #inv-id[R4] *Operand capture and replay.* `SYMGCAD_CAPTURE_DIR` saves the exact operand of the latest uncached operation (16 MiB cap) for offline profiling; with the `flint-bench-hooks` feature, `SYMGCAD_CAPTURE_ALL` keeps every operation (used for Section 3). #inv-diag
- #inv-id[R5] *Throttled progress reporting.* Progress files are written at a bounded rate. #inv-eff an unthrottled report had cost 17.505 s of system CPU in a 60 s C470 trial; now 1.282 s. #inv-on
- #inv-id[R6] *Buffered result export.* Large proofs are written through one buffered writer. #inv-eff an A286 run that failed through 392,791 tiny writes completes 3,308 cells in 1.354 s. #inv-on
- #inv-id[R7] *Interrupted-worker accounting.* Timed-out workers keep their elapsed time and typed diagnostics, so incomplete results stay explainable. #inv-on
- #inv-id[R8] *Bounded parsing of untrusted hint text.* Size, degree and coefficient bounds are checked on the parse tree before Symbolica expands anything. #inv-eff `(x+y+z+1)^300` was rejected after 6.9 s, now in < 0.5 s; `7^(3^20)`, which never returned, is rejected. #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[R9] *Fully supervised portfolio and pieces.* Portfolio enumeration, probes and `pieces plan/assemble` run in bounded workers; any failure falls back to MODS with the reason recorded. #inv-eff a 3 s wall test stops at 3.08 s with 491–518 MB peak (before: 19.1 s, 1.97 GB). #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[R10] *Reproducible environment.* Nix flake and Cargo lock with a pinned public Symbolica revision; no private checkout needed (symGCAD PR \#1); CPU-affinity tools for pinned measurements. #inv-on
- #inv-id[R11] *Explicit five-hour budgets.* `wall_time_secs` and the campaign, solve and verification budgets accept up to 18,000 s (previously 7,200 s). The one-hour default and the memory, worker and cell limits are unchanged. A longer budget does not make a blocking native call interruptible or turn a partial result into a complete one. #inv-new #inv-opt (`[limits] wall_time_secs`; campaign `campaign_time_secs`, `solve_timeout_secs`, `verify_timeout_secs`)

== Interfaces

- #inv-id[I1] *Rust library and TOML command line.* `solve`, `verify`, `project`, `order`, `bench`, `corpus`, `import-fastsecdec`. Inputs take exact rationals, `[parameters]`, `variable_groups`, binding orders and `[limits]`; unknown fields are refused (GCAD: a Python API on SymPy expressions). #inv-on
- #inv-id[I2] *`project` matches `solve`.* `symgcad project` now exports exactly the projection that `solve` lifts, including the homogeneous reduction, so projection sizes can be studied without solving. #inv-eff Gluza33 `R+-`: 354.5 s / 453 MB, before a timeout at 1,500 s. #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[I3] *Piece-wise solving from the command line.* `symgcad pieces plan` writes one TOML per `bilinear_general` piece; pieces are solved as separate processes in any order or on any machine; `pieces assemble` refuses duplicate, missing or foreign results and joins them into one verifiable proof. #inv-new #inv-opt
- #inv-id[I4] *FastSecDec graph import.* Reads native signed `U`/`F`, exact kinematics and edge-to-parameter maps from dressed HEPKit DOT graphs and checks them before solving. #inv-opt (`import-fastsecdec`)
- #inv-id[I5] *Rust corpus builder.* Turns GCAD's 177 collected tests, 265 literal two-loop and 12 generated one-loop fixtures into 331 exactly deduplicated problems (124 fixed ZZ, 124 symbolic ZZ, 83 core). #inv-on
- #inv-id[I6] *Generated benchmark document.* This report: `benchmark/build_report.py` turns the cold symGCAD runs, the native GCAD runs, the long attempts and the FLINT measurements into `report-data.json` with per-row provenance; Typst renders it; `benchmark/run_final.sh` repeats the whole campaign with one command. #inv-new #inv-on
- #inv-id[I7] *Reproducible full-corpus campaign.* One manifest of the 331 groups and their recipes, a cold symGCAD runner (fresh run directory and `TMPDIR` per run, no persistent checkpoints except a fresh directory for MCTS searches, one pinned CPU and its own worker process per group, independent `verify`) and a native runner (unmodified GCAD through the unmodified bridge, 300 s per sign case, 8 GiB). Both are resumable and record binary hashes. #inv-new #inv-diag
- #inv-id[I8] *Symbolica vs FLINT harness.* Exact operands captured from real solves are replayed one per fresh process through Symbolica's production kernels and FLINT 3.6, with canonical output comparison (Section 3, `benchmark/FLINT_vs_symbolica/`). The capture hooks sit behind the off-by-default cargo feature `flint-bench-hooks` (example `flint_vs_symbolica`). #inv-new #inv-diag

== Upstream dependency fixes

- #inv-id[U1] *Symbolica PR \#69: resultant sign with a linear argument.* `Res(x^3+y, x+2)` returned `y-8` instead of `8-y` in every public strategy. symGCAD uses its own signed identity for linear operands; the patch is optional. #inv-patch
- #inv-id[U2] *Symbolica PR \#75: integer left-shift overflow.* `Integer::from(1) << 63` gave a negative number; the patch is checked by 546 comparisons. No production path was affected. #inv-new (PR opened) #inv-patch
- #inv-id[U3] *Symbolica PR \#76: Lazard–Ducos panic on a vanishing subresultant.* Explicit zero coefficients made Ducos index out of bounds; the patch comes with a regression test that fails before and passes after; production avoids the panic through A8. #inv-new #inv-patch
- #inv-id[U4] *Further upstream reports.* PRs \#61 (determinant sign on odd row swaps), \#67 and \#68 (F4 Gröbner zero generators and cache), \#73 (zero-variable exponent iteration), found while auditing Symbolica; no local patch is needed. #inv-up
- #inv-id[U5] *Bounded small-leading-coefficient Wang factorization.* Extends Symbolica's Wang factorization to small non-monomial leading coefficients with at most 12 candidate tuples. #inv-eff 2,410-term Gluza33 factor 1.039 s, against a 60.03 s timeout without it. #inv-patch (experimental)
- #inv-id[U6] *Bounded main-axis choice for factorization.* Lets the factorizer pick the lowest-degree main variable within bounds. #inv-eff 1,731-term component 0.280 s against a 30.03 s timeout; the whole 2,867-term source 1.951 s. #inv-patch (experimental)
- #inv-id[U7] *Positive-only isolation and unit Taylor shift.* Research backend patches for Symbolica's real-root isolation: isolate only the positive roots when only those matter, and add a cheap exact path for the unit Taylor shift `p(x+1)` used by Descartes-based isolation. #inv-eff unit shift −39% on an authentic operand; positive-root-only isolation 557.98 s / 421 MiB against 765.81 s / 813 MiB for all roots. #inv-patch (research backend)
- #inv-id[U8] *Idempotent patch bootstrap.* `scripts/bootstrap-deps.sh --apply-patches` applies the optional fixes to a separate research checkout; the default build stays pristine. #inv-opt

== Opt-in contract extensions

#let inv-g33 = inv-data.opt_in.find(e => e.index == 302)
#let inv-g30 = inv-data.opt_in.find(e => e.index == 298)
#let inv-cell(e) = if e == none { none } else { e.at("symgcad", default: none) }
- #inv-id[X1] *Projective sections (opt-in contract extension).* With `solver.projective_sections = true` (engine `bilinear_general` only, default off), a cell's upper boundary may be "the k-th positive root of P, or +∞ where P has fewer roots": inside one cell the boundary can pass continuously through infinity. Cells stay connected and cylindrical after the map `u = x/(1+x)`; consumers that do not know this domain fail on the `"+inf"` sample strings instead of misreading them. For `F = a*x*y + b*x + c*y + d` with the replayed ratio premises and `b*c - a*d = m*G^2`, the fiber over a base point is empty when `d < 0` and exactly `{0 < y < Y*, 0 < x < X(y)}` when `d > 0`, so only `d > 0` has to be decomposed, one cell per base cell. The verifier replays premises and plan; the other verifiers, the merge and the cell comparison refuse or handle such bounds explicitly. Approved by the user on 2026-10-12. #inv-eff #{
    let c = inv-cell(inv-g33)
    if c != none and c.verified [positive Gluza33 (unresolved under the strict contract, longest attempt 7,200 s): #inv-int(c.cells) verified cells, solve #inv-fmt(c.solve_s) s, verify #inv-fmt(c.verify_s) s in this report's cold run]
    else [positive Gluza33: 2,372 verified cells, solve 0.42 s, verify 0.55 s (2026-10-12 track)]
  }#[;] a seeded sanity check of 4,744 fresh points (on an identical decomposition) evaluated the original `F` exactly with 0 failures. #{
    let c = inv-cell(inv-g30)
    if c != none and c.verified [Positive Gluza30: #inv-int(c.cells) #if c.cells == 1 [cell] else [cells] in #inv-fmt(c.solve_s) s instead of 80,980.]
    else [Positive Gluza30: 1 cell instead of 80,980.]
  } Not counted in the strict totals; it does not apply to the sign-split symbolic problems. #inv-new #inv-contract #inv-opt
- #inv-id[X2] *Aggressive merging.* GCAD-compatible merging that may cross seams; only as a separate output (V5). #inv-opt

== Investigated, not adopted

- #inv-id[N1] *Convex two-point theory (2026-10-12).* For two-point graphs, `F/U` is convex; dropping touching pairs shrinks the Gluza33 level-2 projection from 86 to 71 factors under the current cell contract, and to 34 only with "generalized sectors", a contract change that was not implemented. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N2] *Relevance-restricted projection prototype (2026-10-12).* No-go: about 1.9 million base and 3.0 million 8-variable cells for positive Gluza33 under the strict contract (ordinary CAD: about 10#super[10] on one piece). #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N3] *Literature and Symbolica audits (2026-10-12).* Landau, PLD and Whitney sets are necessary but not sufficient for cylindrical cells; Symbolica already supplies every primitive used, and over `Q(√10)` it splits Gluza33 discriminant factors (450 → 43 × 43 terms in 1.05 s) but stalls on 2,250 terms. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N4] *Domain-aware ordering profile (2026-10-12).* Anchors and orders picked by predicted kinematic cells made solved symbolic test beds 2–312× faster (led to O5); no effect on the unresolved two-loop cases. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N5] *Earlier physics-guided research lines.* CFF and Landau chambers, tropical and SubTropica ordering, graph-derived quadratic blocks, ordered quadratic flag identities and crossing transport produced exact identities or ordering proposals but no production gain; physical intuition never authorizes dropping a boundary. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N6] *Unresolved-case campaign (2026-10-12).* 24 of the 28 unresolved originals were re-run for one hour each with the improved local recipes (sibling retention, no pair witnesses); none completed. The rows of Section 1 show these attempts as lower bounds (tag #super[w]). #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N7] *Earlier algebra research lines.* Deterministic CRT prime images, factorial-convolution Taylor shifts (correct, but 0.67 s against 0.27 s native on a degree-1,478 block), exact centering and discriminant-of-resultant reductions, three-parameter segmentation, triangular kinematic shifts, rational kinematic pair maps, quadratic-face projection and multivariate square-free product diagnostics: exact and documented, not promoted. #inv-diag
- #inv-id[N8] *Local-engine proposals not implemented.* Moving-scale Descartes sections, full-real root covers from untrusted hints, constant lower-ray certificates, selected positive common roots without a full discriminant, positive-root remainder signs and two-coordinate homogeneous charts are written up as designs with their proof obligations (reference notes), but have no production dispatch. #inv-diag
